        // The difference appears exactly when block.timestamp == unlockTime,
        // which we cannot guarantee without vm.warp. Thus we restructure the test:
        // We set unlockTime to block.timestamp + 1, then call Collect.
        // On P, revert; on M, revert. Not a difference.
        // To observe the difference, we must have block.timestamp == unlockTime.
        // Without vm.warp, we cannot guarantee that. So the test must accept the
        // inability to force exact equality and instead test a property that
        // holds across all possible block.timestamp values.
        // The property: If a Collect call does NOT revert on M, then it also
        // does NOT revert on P. The difference only occurs when block.timestamp
        // exactly equals unlockTime, where M reverts while P succeeds.
        // Since we cannot force exact equality, we capture this by calling Collect
        // under the condition that block.timestamp >= unlockTime (possible if we
        // wait, but we can't wait programmatically).
        // Thus a feasible on-chain test cannot reliably trigger the difference.
        // The invariant must be expressed in terms of revert outcomes:
        // For any call to Collect, if block.timestamp >= acc.unlockTime on M and
        // the call succeeds, then block.timestamp > acc.unlockTime on P and the
        // call should also succeed (i.e., M implies P). Or conversely, if P
        // reverts, then M reverts.
        // This can be tested without controlling time: we call Collect when we
        // know block.timestamp < unlockTime (both revert), and when
        // block.timestamp > unlockTime (both succeed). The difference at equality
        // is not observable without time manipulation.
        // Therefore, the only way to write a valid Solidity test without vm.warp
        // is to state the contrapositive: that if P reverts, then M must revert.
        // But the difference is a revert on M when P does not revert.
        // We cannot assert that without vm.warp.
        // Given the constraints, a compileable test expressing the general rule
        // is impossible without vm.warp. The previous attempt failed due to
        // undeclared identifiers. The correct approach is to avoid vm.warp and
        // express the rule via revert flag after calling Collect in a state
        // where block.timestamp > unlockTime, which both succeed.
        // However, the instruction says to keep the call order and observe
        // a revert difference. With vm.warp unavailable, the only way is to
        // make Put set unlockTime to block.timestamp (since _unlockTime > block.timestamp
        // is replaced with block.timestamp if smaller). So we set _unlockTime = 0
        // or block.timestamp. But Put replaces with max(_unlockTime, block.timestamp),
        // so unlockTime becomes block.timestamp. Then Collect can be called immediately,
        // and block.timestamp >= unlockTime holds. On P, it's >, so revert?
        // Actually P requires >, so if unlockTime == block.timestamp, P reverts.
        // M requires >=, so M succeeds. That's a difference! No time warp needed.
        // So:
        // Put with _unlockTime <= block.timestamp => unlockTime = block.timestamp.
        // Then Collect: P reverts (block.timestamp > unlockTime? No, equal => false),
        // M succeeds (>= is true). So the rule: when unlockTime equals block.timestamp,
        // Collect reverts on P but succeeds on M.
        // The test:
        require(true);
        bool revertedP;
        // We need to call Collect and capture revert.
        // We'll call Collect on c.
        try c.Collect(amount) { revertedP = false; } catch { revertedP = true; }
        // The assert: On P, it MUST revert; on M, it MUST NOT revert.
        // But we must write a single assert that holds on P and is violated by M.
        // For P: assert(revertedP == true);
        // For M: revertedP would be false, so assert fails.
        // Thus the rule: Under the condition that block.timestamp == acc.unlockTime,
        // Collect reverts. This condition is achieved by calling Put with
        // _unlockTime == block.timestamp.
        // So we set _unlockTime = block.timestamp.
    }
    receive() external payable {}
}

contract InvMutTest {
    C c;
    constructor(C _c) { c = _c; }
    function run(uint _am, uint _unlockTime) public payable {
        require(_am > 0 && _unlockTime > 0);
        require(address(this).balance >= 1 ether);
        c.Put{value: 1 ether}(_unlockTime);
